From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 18:27:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] sealed init: repair the arena reservation a
 refused commit tore
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Patch 0015 sorted the failures a sealed runtime meets into two classes
and 0016 closed the enumeration: an allocation the runtime cannot get for
itself is a spin, and a malloc the program cannot get is the program's --
a report or, under allocator_may_return_null, a NULL.  There is one
refusal that is both at once.  The line this patch
implements is: the sanitizer's own failures are unreachable; the program's
may-fail outcomes stay visible.

The site is the primary allocator's user-region commit.  The arena is one
PROT_NONE reservation (ReservedAddressRange::Init; 128GiB on riscv64, 4TiB
on x86_64 and aarch64, plus one size class of alignment slack), and each
size class commits its region into it kUserMapSize at a time --
primary64.h, PopulateFreeArray, through MapWithCallback and
ReservedAddressRange::Map: mmap(addr, 256KiB, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_FIXED).  MmapFixedImpl tolerates ENOMEM on
that call and returns null; MapWithCallback returns false, PopulateFreeArray
returns false, and asan_allocator.cpp's Allocate answers as
allocator_may_return_null says, NULL or ReportOutOfMemory.  That is the
program's may-fail outcome and it is right: the plain build's malloc fails
on the same budget, and 0015 left it where it was on purpose.

What was not seen is that the refusal is destructive.  mmap_region in
mm/mmap.c unmaps whatever MAP_FIXED's range holds before it charges the new
pages ("Unmap any existing mapping in the area", then "Private writable
mapping: check memory availability"), so an ENOMEM from the charge leaves
[addr, addr + 256KiB) unmapped: the reservation has a hole where the commit
was asked for.  The arena's invariant -- every byte committed or reserved,
so that nothing else can be placed there -- is broken.  An unhinted mmap
made later, the runtime's or the program's, can be placed in the hole, and
the class's next commit maps over it with MAP_FIXED, unchecked: two things
at one address, and no report.  In the model these binaries are verified
against, ENOMEM on a commit followed by a grant in the hole is an admitted
run, so it is a run the sealed binary has to be correct on.

One function, ReservedAddressRange::Map in sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp,
which both commits reach -- the region data at primary64.h's
PopulateFreeArray and the free array at EnsureFreeArraySpace.  On the null,
a sealed binary re-reserves the same range, with MmapFixedImpl's rounding:
PROT_NONE, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_NORESERVE|MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE,
the flags the shadow is reserved with since 0019, and only the address
asked for is accepted.  Then it returns the null it was going to return,
so the NULL reaches the program.  Any other answer -- EEXIST because
something is there already, an errno, a kernel that read the flag as a
hint and mapped elsewhere -- is the arena's invariant not restored, the
runtime's own failure, and takes SealedHangOnAllocationFailure: the spin.
There is no spin on the NULL path itself.  The free-array commit's null
is already a spin at its ReturnToAllocator caller (0016) and stays one:
repair, then spin.  The re-reserve is not counted against mmap_limit_mb,
as the reservation it restores was not.  Unsealed behaviour is unchanged.

The suite gains three cases on one program -- sealed_commit_refused and
its _norepair and _occupied twins -- behind the seccomp allow-list with
the commit picked out by shape: PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, exactly
MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_FIXED, exactly kUserMapSize.  Every user and
free-array commit ASan's configuration makes is that size (the cache asks
for at most 64KiB of chunks at a time, so RoundUpTo(., kUserMapSize) is
always one step), and nothing else the sealed runtime maps is.  A seccomp
ERRNO refusal would not do: the kernel performs nothing, the reservation
stays whole, and the repair's MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE would be answered EEXIST
by the kernel itself.  So the wrapper answers SECCOMP_RET_TRAP and the
program's SIGSYS handler does what mmap_region does -- unmaps the range,
returns ENOMEM -- and the hole is reproduced rather than assumed.  With
the re-reserve left to the kernel, malloc returns NULL, the program's own
MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE probe of the range answers EEXIST, and it exits 0;
with the re-reserve killed by the filter, the sealed build dies by SIGSYS
after its start line, which is the call being made; with the re-reserve
answered EEXIST, it spins.  The unsealed build is the control in all
three, killed at start-up as in every refused case.
---
 .../sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp | 59 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 57 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp
index 975d7c0..47f878d 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp
@@ -439,11 +439,66 @@ uptr ReservedAddressRange::Init(uptr size, const char *name, uptr fixed_addr) {
   return reinterpret_cast<uptr>(base_);
 }
 
+#if SANITIZER_LINUX
+#  ifndef MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE
+#    define MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE 0x100000
+#  endif
+// A MAP_FIXED commit the kernel refused with ENOMEM is not a no-op.  Linux
+// unmaps whatever the range held before it charges the new pages
+// (mm/mmap.c, mmap_region: "Unmap any existing mapping in the area" comes
+// before "Private writable mapping: check memory availability"), so when the
+// charge is what fails, [addr, addr + size) is left unmapped.  For the primary
+// allocator that range was part of the PROT_NONE reservation
+// ReservedAddressRange::Init took for the whole arena, and the allocator's
+// invariant -- every byte of the arena is committed or reserved, so nothing
+// else can be placed there -- has a hole in it: an unhinted mmap made later,
+// by the runtime or the program, can land in the hole, and the next commit
+// of that size class would map over it with MAP_FIXED, silently.
+//
+// Two different things happen at that refusal, and they are kept apart.  The
+// null MmapFixedOrDieOnFatalError returns is the program's outcome: the
+// allocator hands it up as a malloc that failed, which under
+// allocator_may_return_null is the NULL the program's plain build can also
+// get from its malloc, and that stays visible -- no spin here.  The hole is
+// the runtime's: a sealed binary puts the reservation back, exactly the range
+// the commit tore, PROT_NONE with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, which the kernel
+// refuses with EEXIST where MAP_FIXED would have replaced.  Only the address
+// asked for is accepted, which also covers a kernel old enough to read the
+// flag as a hint.  Any other answer means the arena's invariant could not be
+// restored, and that is the runtime failing to get memory for itself: the
+// spin of patch 0015 (SealedHangOnAllocationFailure).  Unsealed behaviour is
+// unchanged.
+//
+// The re-reserve is not counted against mmap_limit_mb, as the reservation it
+// restores was not, and the failed commit incremented nothing.  The rounding
+// is MmapFixedImpl's, so the range is the one the kernel was asked for.
+static void SealedRepairReservation(uptr fixed_addr, uptr size) {
+  if (!SealedInit())
+    return;
+  size = RoundUpTo(size, GetPageSizeCached());
+  fixed_addr = RoundDownTo(fixed_addr, GetPageSizeCached());
+  uptr res = internal_mmap(reinterpret_cast<void *>(fixed_addr), size,
+                           PROT_NONE,
+                           MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANON | MAP_NORESERVE |
+                               MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE,
+                           -1, 0);
+  if (res != fixed_addr)
+    SealedHangOnAllocationFailure();
+}
+#endif
+
 // Uses fixed_addr for now.
 // Will use offset instead once we've implemented this function for real.
 uptr ReservedAddressRange::Map(uptr fixed_addr, uptr size, const char *name) {
-  return reinterpret_cast<uptr>(
-      MmapFixedOrDieOnFatalError(fixed_addr, size, name));
+  void *res = MmapFixedOrDieOnFatalError(fixed_addr, size, name);
+#if SANITIZER_LINUX
+  // Null is the one tolerated failure, ENOMEM on the MAP_FIXED commit; every
+  // other errno died inside the wrapper.  The commit may have torn the
+  // reservation it was made inside: see SealedRepairReservation.
+  if (UNLIKELY(!res))
+    SealedRepairReservation(fixed_addr, size);
+#endif
+  return reinterpret_cast<uptr>(res);
 }
 
 uptr ReservedAddressRange::MapOrDie(uptr fixed_addr, uptr size,
